  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 224 AM CDT Sun Sep 20 2026\n\nThe flood watch has been extended until 18z today. Periods of\nshowers and thunderstorms are expected to continue through at\nleast mid morning for much of the watch area. The main surface\nlow won't reach central IN until this afternoon and this may\nkeep persistent moderate/heavy rains across parts of the area\nthrough midday. And even from this short(er) distance, there is\nstill some uncertainty where the heaviest rains may fall. The\ncurrent activity may favor the I-80 corridor in the next few\nhours and the next wave over eastern IA may favor areas along \nand north of I-80 with perhaps later waves this morning favoring\nareas south of I-80. With all this in mind, opted to extend the\nflood watch through 18z and as trends emerge, the watch can be \ncancelled as needed later this morning. As for rainfall amounts,\nthere could be one or two swaths of 3-5 inches of rain by the \ntime it ends later today, which could lead to flash flooding.\n\nShowers and scattered thunderstorms may still be moving across\nthe southeast cwa later this afternoon into early this evening \nbefore precipitation finally comes to an end. There may be \nlingering showers tonight and perhaps a few showers on Monday, \nbut only isolated coverage expected.\n\nFog developed over southern Lake Michigan Saturday evening and\nbriefly moved onshore along the IL shore. Webcams suggest the\nvisibilities have improved right near Lake Michigan and as\nnortherly winds slowly increase through the morning, this fog\nmay lift into a low cloud ceiling. Fog over Lake Michigan is\nalso expected to slowly lift as well, though perhaps not until \nthe winds increase further later this morning.\n\nStrong northeast winds will develop today and remain strong\nthrough Wednesday producing high waves and dangerous swimming\nconditions at Lake Michigan beaches. \n\nStill quite a bit of uncertainty for a possible upper low and\nshower chances this week. Temperatures look to be well below \nnormal for the end of September with high temps mainly in the \n60s through Thursday. cms",
